{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","statement":"You can check the database connection's alias via schema_editor.connection.alias inside a RunPython operation to determine which database a migration should run against.","source_span":"you can check the database connection's alias inside a ``RunPython`` operation by looking at the ``schema_editor.connection.alias`` attribute"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","statement":"Hints passed to a RunPython operation are passed as **hints to the allow_migrate method of database routers.","source_span":"You can also provide hints that will be passed to the :meth:`allow_migrate` method of database routers as ``**hints``"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","statement":"If a RunPython or RunSQL operation only affects one model, it's good practice to pass model_name as a hint, especially for reusable and third-party apps.","source_span":"If your ``RunPython`` or ``RunSQL`` operation only affects one model, it's good practice to pass ``model_name`` as a hint"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","statement":"Applying a plain migration that adds a unique non-nullable field to a table with existing rows raises an error because the default value is generated only once, breaking the unique constraint.","source_span":"Applying a \"plain\" migration that adds a unique non-nullable field to a table with existing rows will raise an error because the value used to populate existing rows is generated only once, thus breaking the unique constraint."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","statement":"The recommended process adds the field with default=uuid.uuid4 and unique=True, runs makemigrations, then generates two empty migration files by running makemigrations myapp --empty twice.","source_span":"Add the field on your model with ``default=uuid.uuid4`` and ``unique=True``... Generate two empty migration files for the same app by running ``makemigrations myapp --empty`` twice."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","statement":"In the first (auto-generated) migration file, unique=True is changed to null=True to create an intermediary null field and defer the unique constraint until values are populated.","source_span":"Change ``unique=True`` to ``null=True`` -- this will create the intermediary null field and defer creating the unique constraint until we've populated unique values on all the rows."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","statement":"The second empty migration file (e.g. 0005_populate_uuid_values.py) uses a RunPython operation to generate a unique UUID value for each existing row.","source_span":"In the first empty migration file, add a ... ``RunPython`` ... operation to generate a unique value (UUID in the example) for each existing row."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","statement":"The last migration file (e.g. 0006_remove_uuid_null.py) changes the AddField operation to AlterField with default=uuid.uuid4 and unique=True.","source_span":"Copy the ``AddField`` operation from the auto-generated migration (the first of the three new files) to the last migration, change ``AddField`` to ``AlterField``"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","statement":"There is a race condition where objects created after the AddField operation and before the RunPython operation will have their original UUIDs overwritten.","source_span":"Objects created after the ``AddField`` and before ``RunPython`` will have their original ``uuid``’s overwritten."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","statement":"On databases that support DDL transactions (SQLite and PostgreSQL), migrations run inside a transaction by default.","source_span":"On databases that support DDL transactions (SQLite and PostgreSQL), migrations will run inside a transaction by default."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","statement":"Setting the atomic attribute to False on a Migration class prevents that migration from running inside a transaction (a non-atomic migration).","source_span":"you may want to prevent a migration from running in a transaction by setting the ``atomic`` attribute to ``False``"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","statement":"Within a non-atomic migration, parts can still be executed inside a transaction using transaction.atomic() or by passing atomic=True to RunPython.","source_span":"It's possible to execute parts of the migration inside a transaction using :func:`~django.db.transaction.atomic` or by passing ``atomic=True`` to ``RunPython``."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How can a Django migration determine which database it is running against when using multiple databases?","answer":"By checking the database connection's alias via schema_editor.connection.alias inside a RunPython operation."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What error occurs if you apply a plain migration that adds a unique non-nullable field to a table with existing rows?","answer":"It raises an error, because the default value used to populate existing rows is generated only once, which breaks the unique constraint."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","fact_refs":[5],"question":"In the recommended approach for adding a unique field to a table with existing data, what should unique=True be changed to in the first migration file, and why?","answer":"It should be changed to null=True, which creates an intermediary null field and defers creating the unique constraint until unique values have been populated on all rows."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What race condition exists in the multi-step process for adding a unique field with existing rows?","answer":"Objects created after the AddField operation but before the RunPython operation will have their original UUID values overwritten."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","fact_refs":[9],"question":"Which databases run Django migrations inside a transaction by default, and why?","answer":"SQLite and PostgreSQL, because they support DDL transactions."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","fact_refs":[10],"question":"How do you prevent a Django migration from running inside a transaction?","answer":"Set the atomic attribute to False on the Migration class."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_writing-migrations","fact_refs":[11],"question":"How can you still run part of a non-atomic Django migration inside a transaction?","answer":"Use django.db.transaction.atomic(), or pass atomic=True to the RunPython operation."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_index","statement":"Django's Tutorials take users through a series of steps to create a web application, aimed at people new to Django or web application development.","source_span":"Tutorials </intro/index>` take you by the hand through a series of steps to create a web application. Start here if you're new to Django or web application development."}
